description:
# Establish the WCAG 2.2 AA baseline, manual verification protocol, and automated supplements

**Epic:** `am-ep-accessibility-wrk` · **Batch:** batch-a · **Priority:** P0 · **Plan sections:** §1.8, §16.2, §17.4, §17.6, §19.7 (traceability only)

## Background

The target is **WCAG 2.2 AA with manual verification of mathematics, keyboard-operated instruments, focus restoration, contrast, zoom, screen readers, touch, and reduced motion; automated checks alone are insufficient.** W3C's supplemental cognitive guidance is followed as guidance, not claimed as conformance. Acceptance asks whether a visitor can perform the intended reasoning. This bead lays the foundation every component is built on from Batch A onward, so accessibility is never retrofitted in the publication finish.

WCAG 2.2 AA means every Level A and AA success criterion, not a chosen subset. This bead therefore maps all 55 of them to a rule and a check, an owning bead, or a recorded not-applicable reason with the change that would make it apply. It also names the criteria this site is most likely to break: meaning carried by color in colorized equations (1.4.1), dragging (2.5.7), pinch and rotate gestures in 3D studios and the facsimile viewer (2.5.1), and typed-value errors (3.3.1–3.3.3).

## Scope

In scope:
- shared layout accessibility (skip link, landmarks, heading rules, language attributes, page titles);
- focus styles and the rule that focus is never obscured;
- target sizes and pointer rules;
- reduced-motion handling and audio defaults;
- reflow, text spacing, hover and focus content, color, and contrast rules;
- predictability, consistency, and error conventions;
- status-message conventions;
- the WCAG 2.2 criteria map;
- lint and automated checks with concrete algorithms;
- the manual verification protocol (including mathematics tasks) and recording template;
- an agent-executable pilot.

Out of scope:
- action contracts (`am-a11y-action-contracts-k75g`);
- graph descriptions (`am-a11y-graph-descriptions-vxe1`);
- reading-only settings (`am-a11y-reading-only-6wwd`);
- spoken forms and the term explorer (`am-eq-spoken-forms-w4f`);
- typed-value entry and its out-of-domain messages (`am-inst-parameter-controls-cmj9`, which implements the error rules below for parameters);
- human-run screen-reader and magnification sessions (`am-bm-slice-a11y-codesign-3xuq`, `am-a11y-disabled-reader-rounds-jkjr`);
- the accessibility statement page (`am-design-sources-about-zumd`);
- the browser harness itself (`am-test-e2e-harness-bqmh`).

## Requirements

- **Criteria map.** `docs/accessibility/wcag-22-map.yaml` lists every Level A and AA success criterion of WCAG 2.2: 31 at Level A and 24 at Level AA, 55 in all (4.1.1 Parsing is obsolete in 2.2 and is not listed). Each entry records its id, name, and level and exactly one of:
  - the implementation rule and check in this bead;
  - the owning bead and its check (for example 2.5.7 Dragging Movements through `am-a11y-action-contracts-k75g`, 1.1.1 for canvases through `am-a11y-graph-descriptions-vxe1`, and 3.3.1–3.3.3 for parameter entry through `am-inst-parameter-controls-cmj9`);
  - `not-applicable`, with the reason and the change that would make it apply.
- **Success criteria implemented and checked here** (each mapped to an implementation rule and a check):
  - Already named: 1.1.1 Non-text Content; 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; 1.3.4 Orientation; 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum); 1.4.4 Resize Text; 1.4.10 Reflow; 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast; 1.4.12 Text Spacing; 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus; 2.1.1 Keyboard; 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap; 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide; 2.3.1 Three Flashes or Below Threshold; 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks; 2.4.3 Focus Order; 2.4.7 Focus Visible; 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum); 2.5.3 Label in Name; 2.5.7 Dragging Movements; 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum); 3.1.1 and 3.1.2 Language of Page and of Parts; 3.2.6 Consistent Help (where a help mechanism exists); 3.3.7 Redundant Entry (for multi-step forms such as kitchen-mode entry); 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 4.1.3 Status Messages.
  - Added: 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence; 1.3.3 Sensory Characteristics; 1.4.1 Use of Color; 1.4.2 Audio Control; 1.4.5 Images of Text; 2.1.4 Character Key Shortcuts; 2.4.2 Page Titled; 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context); 2.4.5 Multiple Ways; 2.4.6 Headings and Labels; 2.5.1 Pointer Gestures; 2.5.2 Pointer Cancellation; 3.2.1 On Focus; 3.2.2 On Input; 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation; 3.2.4 Consistent Identification; 3.3.1 Error Identification; 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions; 3.3.3 Error Suggestion; 3.3.4 Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data), which applies to deleting stored reader data and to exercise answers.
  - Recorded `not-applicable`, each with its revisit trigger:
    - 1.2.1–1.2.5: no audio or video content at launch; revisit when reviewed narration or any recorded media ships;
    - 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose: no field collects information about the reader; revisit if a contact or feedback form collects it;
    - 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable: no time limits, because timers are prohibited;
    - 2.5.4 Motion Actuation: no device-motion input;
    - 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum): no accounts.
- **Structure**: skip link to main content; `header`, `nav`, `main`, `aside`, and `footer` landmarks; one `h1` per page and ordered headings; `lang="en"` on the document and `lang="de"` on German passages, with correct switching inside mixed sentences; DOM order matches reading order in every face, including the parallel German and English faces and the companion panel.
- **Titles, links, and headings**: each route has a unique descriptive title (paper, section, and face where applicable); link text names its destination in context (for example "Back to §4, paragraph 2"), never "here" or "more"; headings and control labels describe their topic or purpose; every paper section is reachable through navigation, a table of contents, and search.
- **Focus**: visible focus indicators with at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors; focused elements never fully hidden behind sticky headers, companions, or laboratories; focus restoration after closing any overlay or clarification to the element that opened it.
- **Targets and input**:
  - pointer targets are at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels or meet the spacing exception;
  - every dragging action has a single-pointer or non-drag alternative, implemented through action contracts;
  - every multipoint or path-based gesture (pinch or rotate in a 3D studio, facsimile zoom) has a single-pointer alternative;
  - custom controls commit on the up event, and moving off the control before release cancels;
  - no keyboard traps; visible labels are contained in accessible names;
  - a single-character keyboard shortcut can be turned off or remapped, or works only while its component has focus.
- **Predictability and consistency**: focus never changes context; changing a control changes content in place (a face or detail switch keeps the reader's anchor) and never navigates away or opens a window without an explicit activation; the shared layout keeps navigation in the same relative order on every route; a function has the same label and icon everywhere (for example "Describe now" and "Load this experiment").
- **Errors and data**:
  - an input error is identified in text next to the input, names the field, and suggests an admissible value or boundary; nothing is silently clamped;
  - clearing locally stored notes, tours, or predictions asks for confirmation or can be undone;
  - exercise checks are reversible: the reader can change an answer and check again, and nothing is recorded as a final result.
- **Hover and focus content**: any content shown on hover or focus is dismissible without moving focus (Escape), hoverable, and persistent until dismissed; no tooltip-only content.
- **Color and sensory characteristics**: color never carries meaning alone. Every colorized equation term, legend entry, plot series, and state also carries a second channel (a text label, term id, pattern, marker, or weight). Instructions never rely on color, shape, position, or sound alone ("the red term", "the plot on the right").
- **Real text**: text and mathematics are real text (KaTeX HTML with MathML), never images of text.
- **Motion and audio**: honor `prefers-reduced-motion` and the site setting; pause random walks and boosts while showing the current state; no parallax or autoplaying decorative motion; animations pausable; nothing flashes more than three times per second; sound starts muted, and any audio longer than 3 seconds has pause and volume controls independent of the system volume.
- **Visual**: reflow at 320 CSS px without two-dimensional page scrolling (local scroll regions allowed only for equations and tables, each keyboard-scrollable and labeled); text-spacing overrides (line height 1.5, paragraph spacing 2 em, letter spacing 0.12 em, word spacing 0.16 em) do not clip or overlap content; text contrast AA and non-text contrast 3:1 using tokens from `am-design-themes-typography-288q`; forced-colors mode keeps focus, selection, and state visible.
- **Status messages**: polite announcements for committed changes through one announcement utility (`src/a11y/announce.ts`); no high-frequency live regions.
- **No-JavaScript readers** get real links, never hydration-dependent buttons.
- **Automated supplements**:
  - Biome accessibility lint rules on; axe-core in end-to-end lanes with zero serious or critical violations.
  - Custom checks with defined algorithms:
    - focus obscuring: for each focusable element after `focus()`, sample its bounding box at nine points with `elementsFromPoint` and fail if every point is covered by a fixed or sticky element outside its ancestors;
    - target size: bounding boxes of interactive elements are at least 24×24 or meet the 24 px spacing exception;
    - text spacing: inject the override stylesheet, then fail on any element whose `scrollHeight` or `scrollWidth` exceeds its client box with `overflow: hidden`, or on overlapping text boxes;
    - `lang`: every German source block carries `lang="de"`;
    - page titles: every route in the build's route manifest has a non-empty title, unique across routes;
    - link text: no link has an empty accessible name or one of "here", "click here", "more", "link";
    - color only: in fixture views, every element styled with a semantic color token also exposes a non-color channel (a visible text label, a `data-term-id` bound to a legend label, or a distinct dash pattern or marker), and a seeded legend distinguished by color alone fails;
    - pointer cancellation: a custom control receiving `pointerdown` followed by `pointercancel`, or `pointerdown` followed by `pointerup` outside its bounds, commits nothing.
- **Manual protocol** (`docs/accessibility/manual-protocol.md`):
  - the assistive-technology matrix: NVDA with Firefox and Chrome; JAWS with Chrome; VoiceOver with Safari on macOS and iOS; TalkBack with Chrome on Android;
  - keyboard only; screen magnification at 200 and 400 percent; forced colors; reduced motion; switch or voice control where available; a refreshable braille display where available;
  - the WAI-ARIA slider pattern's touch warning, tested on devices;
  - scripted reasoning tasks: compare event times, choose a displacement statistic, change a parameter, inspect a conservation balance, explain an equation step;
  - **mathematics tasks**: a displayed formula is announced once through its authored spoken form, with no duplicate announcement from the visual rendering and a redundant label; the term explorer can be entered and left; German and English passages switch speech language correctly; an equation term can be identified without color (forced colors or grayscale) through its label;
  - a pointer task: operate a 3D studio view and the facsimile zoom with a single pointer;
  - a results template recording tester, date, tool versions, task, outcome, barrier, severity, and follow-up bead.
- **Pilot run (agent-executable, recorded honestly)**: on the scaffold and fixture pages, run the automated lanes, a scripted keyboard-only walkthrough, and accessibility-tree snapshot checks, and record them with the template. Screen-reader, magnification, switch, voice-control, and braille results are recorded only by named human testers (`am-bm-slice-a11y-codesign-3xuq`); this bead never records such a pass it did not observe.

## Technical Approach

- `src/app/layout.tsx` accessibility structure, `src/a11y/announce.ts`, `src/a11y/focus.ts`, `src/a11y/reducedMotion.ts`, `src/a11y/pointer.ts` (commit-on-release and cancellation helpers for custom controls), CSS utilities for focus and spacing, and `scripts/a11y/checks.ts` (focus obscuring, target size, text spacing, `lang`, page titles, link text, color only), with lanes registered in `am-test-e2e-harness-bqmh` and logs written through `am-test-logging-standard-l3cp`.
- `docs/accessibility/wcag-22-map.yaml` is validated by `scripts/a11y/wcagMap.test.ts` against a committed list of the 55 WCAG 2.2 Level A and AA criteria, with their ids, names, and levels transcribed from the W3C Recommendation (the source URL and retrieval date recorded in the file).

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] The criteria map lists all 55 Level A and AA criteria of WCAG 2.2 with correct levels; every entry names a rule and check, an owning bead, or a not-applicable reason with its revisit trigger.
- [ ] Layout structure, titles, links, focus, target-size, pointer, shortcut, predictability, error, hover-content, color, motion, audio, reflow, spacing, contrast, language, and announcement rules are implemented on the scaffold and fixture pages, with each criterion this bead owns mapped to a rule and a check.
- [ ] axe-core lanes report zero serious or critical violations on fixture pages.
- [ ] Each custom check passes on clean fixtures and fails on its seeded violation: a sticky header covering a focused link; a 20×20 button; a clipped paragraph under the spacing override; a German passage without `lang`; two routes with the same title; a link named "here"; a legend distinguished by color alone; a control that commits on `pointerdown`.
- [ ] The manual protocol and template exist, including the mathematics tasks, the color-free term identification, the single-pointer task, and the touch slider warning; the agent-executable pilot is recorded; no human-only result is recorded by an agent.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests

- `announce.test.ts`: politeness, debouncing, and exactly one announcement per commit.
- `focus.test.tsx`: restoration after overlays and clarifications, including nested overlays.
- `reducedMotion.test.ts`: the media query and the site setting both pause animation and keep the current state.
- `pointer.test.ts`: commit on release; `pointercancel` and release outside the bounds commit nothing.
- `checks.test.ts`: each custom check detects its seeded violation and passes its clean counterpart.
- `wcagMap.test.ts`: all 55 ids present once with correct levels; 4.1.1 absent; a not-applicable entry without a reason or revisit trigger fails; an entry naming an owning bead must name its check.

### End-to-end tests

Through `am-test-e2e-harness-bqmh`:
- axe lanes on fixture pages;
- tab through fixture pages asserting visible, unobscured focus and logical order;
- 320 px reflow; text-spacing override injection; 200 and 400 percent zoom;
- reduced-motion emulation; forced colors with the color-only check;
- hover content dismissal with Escape;
- the page-title and link-text checks across the route manifest;
- a no-JavaScript lane confirming real links;
- a WebKit lane.

### Structured logging

JSONL through `am-test-logging-standard-l3cp` to `artifacts/test-logs/a11y-baseline/<log-run-id>.jsonl` with `timestamp`, `suite`, `logRunId`, `testId`, `beadId`, `route`, `rule`, `successCriterion`, `violations`, `focusObscuredCount`, `smallTargets`, `spacingClipping`, `duplicateTitles`, `vagueLinks`, `colorOnlyElements`, `reducedMotion`, `jsEnabled`, `browser`, `viewport`, `outcome`, `message`; failures retain a screenshot, trace, DOM snapshot, accessibility-tree snapshot, and console log. Manual results live in `docs/accessibility/runs/`.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- A clean axe report is not accessibility; the manual reasoning tasks decide.
- A subset of success criteria is not a WCAG 2.2 AA baseline; the map keeps unlisted criteria from becoming silently out of scope.
- Colorized equations are the site's signature, which makes 1.4.1 its most likely failure: the second channel is designed in from the start, never added after the palette hardens.
- Participation in manual testing never collects diagnoses or placement scores.
- A local horizontal scroll region for a long equation is allowed; a page that scrolls horizontally at 320 px is not.

## Dependencies

Blocked by:
- `am-scaf-nextjs-app-bu2`: the application layout.
- `am-design-themes-typography-288q`: contrast and focus tokens.
- `am-test-e2e-harness-bqmh`: the browser lanes this bead's acceptance runs on.
- `am-test-logging-standard-l3cp`: JSONL evidence.

Unblocks:
- `am-a11y-graph-descriptions-vxe1`: built on the announcement and focus utilities.
- `am-a11y-reading-only-6wwd`: settings layered on the baseline.
- `am-eq-math-announcement-at-matrix-0al6`: runs the manual protocol's assistive-technology matrix to choose the mathematics announcement pattern.

# Extend browser acceptance to the complete Brownian paper and every instrument contract

**Epic:** `am-ep-brownian-complete-6xe` · **Batch:** batch-c · **Priority:** P1 · **Labels:** testing, e2e · **Plan sections:** §6.5, §8.2, §10.4, §12.6, §16.2, §16.4, §17.4, §17.7 item 9, §19.3 C (traceability only)

## Background

The slice suite proved one continuous journey through §§4–5. The complete paper adds:

- Route A, with its printed variational route and the fair classical rival;
- three Route A instruments;
- two inference instruments;
- the kitchen mode;
- the margin records, the misconception ledger, and the results cards.

The definition of done requires the Playwright vertical slice for the whole paper to be green.

This bead extends the `brownian-slice` suite into a `brownian-complete` suite. The new suite:

- keeps the slice journey unchanged;
- adds a Route A journey;
- adds every new instrument's parameter, refusal, command-class, and permalink contract;
- adds the two noncircularity checks (historical inference, and BM-04's kick-strength probe);
- adds the paper-level lanes: faces, readings, margin, misconceptions, results, print, and no JavaScript.

It runs against real compiled content and real owners. Nothing under test is mocked.

## Scope

**In scope:** the `brownian-complete` suite, its lanes, its helpers, and its logs and evidence.

**Out of scope:**

- The slice suite (`am-bm-slice-e2e-sbqu`), reused unchanged.
- The harness (`am-test-e2e-harness-bqmh`).
- Human reviews.
- Journey II's own suite (`am-disc-journey-ii-chain-pl6u`).

## Requirements

### Route A continuous journey

1. Deep link to the §3 sentence stating $D$ on the English face. Switch to the German and parallel faces, and confirm alignment highlighting keeps the same sentence focused.
2. Open the foundation "Viscosity and Stokes drag" and return to the exact sentence.
3. Open Chain E, switch between the printed variational route and the labeled pedagogical route, then open Chain F and select the cancellation of $K$.
4. Open BM-04 and type forces of +4.047 fN and −10 fN. Run each to steady state and confirm the inferred $D$ is unchanged at $0.42944\,\mu\text{m}^2/\text{s}$ at the golden inputs, and that Chain F's cancellation step is highlighted.
5. Set kick strength $m = 2$: the kinetic decay length reads 2 μm against the osmotic 1 μm, and the weave does not light.
6. Choose BM-04's declared `bm-04:kicks-off` mode: with force, the pile-up at the wall and the branch label; with zero force and a step profile, the step persists.
7. Select the colorized $D$ term and confirm the highlight across equation, table, chain, and sentence.
8. Return to §3 without losing position.

### Instrument contracts (direct numeric entry, refusals, command classes, permalinks)

**BM-02:**
- size independence at equal $n$;
- the dilute boundary ($N_p = 19\,098$ accepted, $19\,099$ refused with its boundary);
- $N_p = 0$;
- the classical-expectation model toggle (new run; its label and deciding-evidence note);
- a partition-area change as a measurement change.

**BM-03:**
- stepwise construction by keyboard with Chain G highlights;
- the $N_p = 10^6$ logarithmic view with no nonfinite value;
- the locked cluster (factor $V/V_0$, pressure $k_BT/V$, the §2 output `outside-domain`).

**BM-04:**
- the zero-force `not-applicable` quotient with the relation `symbolic`;
- the `drift-diffusion-unstable` refusal for an over-limit time step;
- the `drift-cfl-exceeded` refusal in the kicks-off branch.

**BM-07:**
- the synthetic interval fixture ($q = 100$, $\widehat D = 0.42944$ gives $[0.331457, 0.578589]$);
- the identifiability view before any interval, in reading and tab order;
- the wrong-radius probe (the data digest is unchanged: an estimator change);
- the combined Bonferroni interval with declared radius coverage;
- the Perrin 1909 provenance card and historical labels;
- `underdetermined` for $M = 1$ with drift estimation;
- the teaching tape `perrins-count` replays to its recorded result.

**BM-08:**
- noise-only at $\sigma = 0.2\,\mu$m: a negative lag covariance, and the naive interval disabled with its reason;
- the CVE point estimates with `not-applicable` interval;
- the known-noise disjoint-pairs interval admitted;
- the latent-path digest unchanged across measurement changes;
- fluid drift creates a new run, stage drift does not;
- off-grid exposure refused.

**Kitchen mode:**
- the constant-frame-rate synthetic video pipeline with stationary-feature clicks yields an admitted pair interval;
- the variable-frame-rate video is refused for timing;
- switching the gas-constant source is an estimator change: the clicks digest is unchanged, the snapshot version advances, `data-run-id` is unchanged, and the wording changes between "independent estimate" (the measured set and Einstein's 1905 set) and "consistency check" (`modern-si-2019`), with the ratio and $\hat k_B$ shown for the latter and no text calling $R$ a printed value;
- the session saves to the registered namespace `am:kitchen:v1`, appears in the data panel, exports, and clears;
- zero outbound requests carrying media, frames, or clicks;
- a CSV import and export round trip, and injection-safe export;
- the mode runs under the production Content-Security-Policy.

**Every instrument:** a permalink round trip restores identical snapshot identities, and seeds above $2^{53}$ survive as decimal strings.

### Noncircularity checks

- **Historical inference.** In BM-07 historical mode, swap the modern constant set for a deliberately altered one. The historical data and results must be bitwise unchanged, and the constant-set access log must show no modern read.
- **Programmed diffusivity.** In BM-04, the $D$ agreement and its weave appear only at kick strength 1, never at other strengths.

Both checks are recorded in the log.

### Paper-level lanes

- **JavaScript disabled:** all faces of the whole paper render R1 text, static MathML, printed worked examples, margin records, and misconception callouts.
- **Print:** complete prose, uncut equations, expanded essential explanations under the landed policy of `am-plat-print-3orn`, and static instrument states. This suite carries no `pendingOn` marker: the policy is a dependency here, so these assertions run rather than reporting `not-available`.
- **Misconception callouts** render inline and on the results face, and each opens its instrument at the declared mode or registered preset the ledger names (for example `bm-04:kicks-off` for entry 1 and `bm-08-noise-only` for entry 8).
- **Margin records** render at their R3 anchors.
- **Results cards** expand, and probe snapshot identities match the instruments.
- **Reading-face HTML** stays within budget, or the fragment fallback works.
- **Viewports and modes:** desktop, tablet, 320 px touch, WebKit, keyboard only, reduced motion, high zoom.

### Evidence and logs

- **Location:** JSONL at `artifacts/test-logs/brownian-complete/<log-run-id>.jsonl`, written through `am-test-logging-standard-l3cp`.
- **Fields:** `timestamp`, `suite`, `testId`, `beadId`, `paper`, `anchor`, `instrumentId`, `instanceId`, `logRunId`, `runId`, `inputRevision`, `acceptedInputRevision`, `snapshotVersion`, `seed` (decimal string), `streamVersion`, `modelVersion`, `artifactDigest`, `executionLabel`, `resultStatus`, `expected`, `actual`, `tolerance`, `comparisonKind`, `outcome`, `durationMs`, `browser`, `viewport`, `reducedMotion`, `jsEnabled`, `message`, plus `lane`, `commandClass`, `constantSetId`, `semanticKind`, and `dataDigest` where relevant.
- **Failure evidence:** screenshots, traces, DOM snapshots, console logs, and snapshot JSON are retained on failure.
- **Canary:** a deliberate failing test proves the evidence path works and is excluded from the green count.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] The Route A journey passes in every applicable lane.
- [ ] All instrument contracts above pass, including refusals, command-class invariants, and permalinks.
- [ ] Both noncircularity checks pass and are logged.
- [ ] Whole-paper print, JavaScript-disabled, margin, misconception, and results lanes pass, with the print lane asserting the landed essential-for-print policy and carrying no pending marker.
- [ ] Logs and failure evidence are produced, the canary proves the failure path, and the suite runs in CI.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests

Helpers, each with its own tests against real fixtures:

- `latentDigest` comparison of snapshot latent paths;
- the constant-set swap fixture, with the altered set's values logged;
- the synthetic video fixture loader, for both frame-rate variants;
- the network-request auditor, which fails on any request whose URL, headers, or body contains media, frame pixels, or click data;
- the tab-order assertion helper for "identifiability before interval";
- the snapshot nonfinite-value walker;
- the address resolver that checks a misconception's declared mode or registered preset against the instrument manifest, failing on a preset written with a colon or a mode written with a hyphen.

### End-to-end tests

The suite as specified, under `e2e/brownian-complete/`.

- Statistical assertions use only prespecified bands from the instrument beads.
- A failure is investigated, never rerun until it passes.

### Structured logging

As specified in Requirements ("Evidence and logs"), with per-lane summaries (`{suite, lane, passed, failed, skipped: 0, durationMs}`) and a run summary that lists every refusal code observed.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- Keep statistical assertions on prespecified bands; investigate failures rather than rerunning.
- The kitchen synthetic videos are test fixtures, labeled synthetic wherever they appear.
- A lane may not be skipped to get green. A lane that cannot run on the CI host is reported as blocked with its reason.
- This suite inherits the slice suite unchanged. If a slice assertion needs to change for the complete paper, change it in the slice bead, not by forking the step here.

## Dependencies

**Blocked by:**

- `am-bm-slice-e2e-sbqu`: the base suite.
- `am-bm-02-osmotic-partition-n13x`, `am-bm-03-configuration-integral-e84v`, `am-bm-04-drift-diffusion-balance-fpow`, `am-bm-07-infer-molecular-number-frf9`, `am-bm-08-measurement-bias-h1ye`: the instruments under test.
- `am-bm-07-kitchen-mode-mays`: the suite exercises the kitchen mode.
- `am-bm-readings-intro-s1-s3-l3tp`: the content under test.
- `am-bm-equations-s1-s3-u6j7`: the journey opens Chains E and F.
- `am-bm-margin-entries-7fa4`: the margin lane.
- `am-bm-misconceptions-2hk1`: the misconception lane.
- `am-bm-results-cards-ft8k`: the results lane.
- `am-plat-print-3orn`: the essential-for-print policy this suite's print lane asserts, and whose pending markers it therefore never carries.

**Unblocks:** `am-dod-brownian-2k4y` (definition-of-done item 9), `am-bm-review-physics-arfv` (the review packet links the latest green run).

# Implement the structural compiler rejections

**Epic:** `am-ep-content-model-9e3` · **Batch:** A · **Priority:** P0 · **Plan sections:** §3.1, §4.4, §6.9, §11.7, §17.3 (traceability only)

## Background

The plan lists what the first compiler must reject (§11.7). The structural subset catches broken references and impossible records before any reader sees them:
- duplicate ids, missing source blocks, broken alignment edges, dangling citations;
- impossible date ordering;
- an English equation block that is not byte-identical to its aligned German block;
- a paper marked complete while required blocks or explanation obligations are absent;
- a hero quote that does not resolve to edition text at its anchor.

These failures are cheap to detect and expensive to discover in production. That is especially true in a bilingual edition, where a single broken alignment edge silently detaches a translation from its source.

## Scope

In scope: registering the structural checks with the compiler, their error messages, and fixtures.

Out of scope, owned elsewhere: symbol, quantity, owner, and math-command checks (`am-cm-checks-semantic-lic`); proof, shelf, and accessibility checks (`am-cm-checks-epistemic-o7n`); manifest completeness, locators, references, frozen ids, and result imports (`am-cm-source-manifest-6qa`); the German-edition-contained-in-ledger test, math token sequence matching, and gloss token checks (`am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1`); review-record coverage of `reviewed` states (`am-edit-review-records-hofz`).

## Requirements

Each check reports `{recordId, file, path, rule, message, repair}` through the compiler's check API:

1. `duplicate-id`: two records share an id within a namespace (source ids within one document of one paper; generic record ids within an entity namespace; equation record ids and argument ids globally).
2. `missing-source-block`: a paper's ordered block list, a translation unit, an alignment edge, an editorial note, or an argument node's source support names a block or sentence that does not exist. Cross-paper `{paper, id}` references are resolved against the named paper.
3. `broken-alignment`:
   - an edge's source sentence is not inside its block;
   - an edge's target translation unit is missing;
   - a character range is out of bounds;
   - a German alignable unit has no alignment edge: a paragraph sentence, or a block-level unit (a section heading `s<n>`, a part heading, a masthead unit, a footnote, `closing-dateline`, `closing-ack`, or `closing-received`), following the alignable-unit rule of `am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`;
   - an English translation unit has no alignment edge;
   - a suffixed translation unit (`s3-p2-s1a`, `s3-fn1a`, `closing-ack-a`) has no sibling (a split needs at least two parts), or an unsuffixed translation unit exists beside suffixed ones for the same source unit.
4. `dangling-citation`: a citation id is referenced by a `reference` inline, an `EditorialNote.sourceSupport`, a card, an argument node's evidence, or a dataset, but not defined.
5. `impossible-date-order`, precision-aware. Each typed date is an interval (a day is one day; `1905-05` is 1–31 May 1905; `1905` is the whole year). A constraint "X is not after Y" fails only when the earliest possible X is later than the latest possible Y. Checked pairs:
   - date-line not after received;
   - received not after issue publication;
   - issue publication not after any later edition;
   - date-line not after submitted.

   So "Bern, Mai 1905" with receipt on 11 May 1905 is consistent, and the dissertation's real dates (dated 30 April 1905, submitted 20 July 1905) pass. A fixture with a date-line of "Bern, Juni 1905" and receipt on 11 May 1905 fails, as does a fixture that swaps the dissertation's date-line and submission date.
6. `equation-not-identical`: an English-face equation block whose serialized mathematical content differs by even one byte from the aligned German equation block. Equations are shared by id wherever possible; any duplicated block must match exactly, with no whitespace or Unicode normalization.
7. `complete-while-missing`: a paper or section marked complete while the source manifest reports required units without reviewed statuses, or while argument nodes in that scope lack coverage obligations.
8. `hero-quote-unresolved`: a hero or pull quote's exact text is not found in the edition text of the declared language at its anchor. Matching collapses runs of whitespace to one space and nothing else: no case folding, and no normalization of quotation marks, dashes, or ligatures. A quote containing an ellipsis must be authored as ordered `segments[]`, each of which must be found in order within the anchor's text.
9. `ledger-marker-in-edition`: edition text contains `--- REVIEWED TRANSCRIPTION PAGE` (the ledger marker pattern `--- REVIEWED TRANSCRIPTION PAGE <n> OF <N> ---`) or any other scan-page furniture recorded for that document by the ledger validator. Page locators belong in the ledger and receipt.
10. `span-digest-mismatch`: a span's stored `normalizedTextDigest` differs from `spanTextDigest` of the record it names, which is the source block for a German span and the translation unit for an English span. It covers `SourceBlock.sentenceSpans[]` entries and every `Alignment` edge endpoint that carries a `range`, both of which gain `blockRevision` and `normalizedTextDigest` in `am-cm-schemas-source-1en`; a block-level edge with no `range` carries no digest and is not checked. The message gives the block id, the sentence id, the stored digest, the computed digest, and the repair (re-measure the spans of this block and bump `blockRevision`). This is the alignment-layer counterpart of `gloss-stale`, and it closes a gap nothing else sees: a paragraph edited without re-measuring its spans keeps offsets that now point at the wrong words, while every id still resolves and every edge still validates. The revision-only counterpart, `span-revision-stale`, is reported separately and belongs to `am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1`.

## Technical Approach

Implement each rule as a registered check in `src/content/checks/structural/` using the compiler's indexes. Date comparison uses the typed date schema from `am-cm-schemas-source-1en` with interval semantics in `src/content/checks/structural/dateIntervals.ts`, which `am-cm-source-manifest-6qa` also uses for import chronology. The digest rule calls `spanTextDigest` from `src/content/schemas/spans.ts` (`am-cm-schemas-source-1en`) and never computes a second digest of its own, so exactly one implementation decides what counts as the same text. Each rule is independent, so one broken record never hides another failure.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] All ten rules are registered and produce errors that fail the build.
- [ ] Each rule has at least one failing fixture and one passing fixture.
- [ ] Messages name the record id, the field path, and a concrete repair.
- [ ] Month-precision comparisons pass "Mai 1905 / 11 May" and fail a genuinely inverted order; the dissertation's real dates pass and the swapped fixture fails.
- [ ] Heading, part-heading, footnote, masthead, and closing units (including `closing-received`) are covered by the alignment rule at block level, and the block-level suffix forms are checked for siblings.
- [ ] `span-digest-mismatch` fails a fixture whose paragraph text was edited without re-measuring its spans, naming the block id, the sentence id, and both digests, and passes once the spans are re-measured and `blockRevision` is bumped.
- [ ] The real (initially empty) corpus passes.

## Test Plan

### Unit and integration tests
`src/content/checks/structural/structural.test.ts`: a base valid fixture corpus plus one mutation per rule, run through the real compiler. Each case asserts the rule id and record id in the error:
- a duplicate paragraph id;
- a translation unit pointing to `s9-p1`, which does not exist;
- a cross-paper reference to `special-relativity#eq-s8-d9`, which does not exist;
- an alignment edge whose range extends beyond the sentence;
- an unaligned German sentence, an unaligned footnote block, an unaligned section heading `s2`, and an unaligned `closing-received`;
- a lone suffixed unit `s2-p1-s3a`, and a lone block-level suffix `s2-fn1a`;
- the citation `pais-1982` referenced by an editorial note but undefined;
- a received date `1905-03-16` before the date-line `1905-03-17`;
- "Bern, Juni 1905" with receipt on 11 May 1905;
- the dissertation fixture with swapped date-line and submission;
- an English equation block with an extra space;
- a paper marked complete with a machine-draft translation;
- a hero quote with one changed word, and an ellipsis quote whose segments appear out of order;
- an edition paragraph containing a ledger page marker;
- a paragraph whose text is edited without re-measuring its `sentenceSpans[]`, and the same mutation applied to an alignment edge endpoint's `range`.

`src/content/checks/structural/dateIntervals.test.ts`: day, month, and year intervals; boundary equality (the same day passes); leap-year months; a year-precision date against a day-precision date.

### End-to-end tests
The quality gate chain runs `bun scripts/build-content.ts --corpus <fixture-mutation>` for every mutation through a test-only entry point and asserts a non-zero exit with the expected rule ids in the JSONL log; the base corpus must exit zero.

### Structured logging
- Findings: `{timestamp, suite: "build-content", logRunId, beadId: "am-cm-checks-structural-lq0", rule, recordId, file, path, expected, actual, message, repair}` lines in `artifacts/test-logs/build-content/<log-run-id>.jsonl` through the shared logger.
- Unit suites log `testId`, `mutation`, `expectedRule`, `actualRules`, `outcome`, and `durationMs`.
- A failing mutation test retains the mutated fixture path in its log line.


**One compiler suite, one directory.** Every check family runs in one compiler process, so the compiler writes a single diagnostics file per run at `artifacts/test-logs/build-content/<log-run-id>.jsonl` with `suite: "build-content"` on every line, following the logger mapping for scripts (the suite is the script name, `scripts/build-content.ts`). The family is the validated field `extra.family`, taken from the registered check. This bead reports under `extra.family: "structural"`. The declared families are `compiler`, `structural`, `semantic`, `epistemic`, `manifest`, `dimension`, `coverage`, `readings`, `audit`, `anachronism`, `exports`, `i18n`, `ids`, `schema`, `notation`, and `voice`, and `registerCheck` refuses any other, so no check can invent a suite name or a second directory. Test suites keep their own names and directories: this bead's unit and integration suite logs `suite: "content-structural-tests"` to `artifacts/test-logs/content-structural-tests/<log-run-id>.jsonl`. No suite writes into another suite's directory, and no path spells the run id in camel case.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- "Byte-identical" is deliberately strict; it prevents a translation workflow from quietly "tidying" a formula.
- Date precision matters: many date-lines give only a month. Compare intervals, never truncated strings.
- Keep checks independent so one broken record does not hide other failures.
- Hero-quote matching must not be forgiving; a curly quote silently replaced by a straight one is still a misquotation.

## Dependencies

Blocked by: `am-cm-compiler-core-oa7` (check API and indexes). `am-cm-schemas-source-1en` supplies the typed date schema, the `SpanAnchor` shape, and `spanTextDigest`; this bead reaches it through `am-cm-compiler-core-oa7`, which depends on it directly, so no separate edge is needed.

Unblocks: `am-edn-alignment-tooling-do1` (builds on alignment rules), `am-cm-audit-scripts-d34` (includes these checks in `verify-content.ts`).

# Define schemas for HistoricalPremise, ArgumentNode, Quantity, Equation, Foundation, Misconception, readings, and the four kinds of meaning

**Epic:** `am-ep-content-model-9e3` · **Batch:** A · **Priority:** P0 · **Plan sections:** §5.1, §5.2, §6.2–6.6, §7.5, §7.6, §9.1, §11.2, §11.4, §11.5, §11.6 (traceability only)

## Background

The organizing unit of this site is an **argument**, not a mechanism. These schemas give arguments, their premises, their equations, and their explanations a typed shape, so the compiler can enforce the epistemic rules that make the site honest:
- four historical statements are not interchangeable (§5.1);
- every premise on the 1904 shelf is a dated card with a `latestYear` (§5.2);
- proofs have acyclic dependency graphs, with historical derivation edges distinguished from modern verification oracles (§11.6);
- every argument node, equation, and displayed quantity carries four separate kinds of meaning that are never compressed into one color (§11.4);
- every explanation carries an authoring contract, so meaning survives a change of representation (§6.6).

Many later beads build directly on these shapes: the quantity registry, the expression tree, the concordance, knowledge cards, journeys, foundations, misconceptions, and the coverage ledger. The field names and enum spellings defined here are the single definitions those beads import. The knowledge-card fields follow the conventions settled with the discovery beads, so a shelf card authored for one journey is the same record the desk, the timeline, and every other journey cite.

Four shared contracts were added in round 2:
- the `authorship` block on every reviewable record (defined in `am-cm-schemas-source-1en`), so a reviewer can be compared with the record's authors;
- the `FoundationLink` record with its return-caption precedence, and prerequisites typed as proof edges or cross-links (consumed by `am-found-library-infra-002t`);
- reading targets typed by kind, following the readings ownership ruling (paragraphs, headings, footnotes, and closings belong to readings beads; equations and derivation steps to equations beads; captions to instrument beads);
- the dimension records the historical unit systems and paper 2's symbolic §2 argument need: electromagnetic CGS dimensions for the light-quanta §8 check as printed, state-dependent dimensions for the configuration integral, and exact scale factors on term bindings for Einstein's $\kappa$.

Round 3 adds five more, each requested by a consuming bead and each additive with a schema-version bump: the authored `recap` on `ArgumentNode` (`am-read-notebook-tde`), which is the pattern the other four follow, the five-part `WorkedExample` on `Foundation` (`am-found-library-infra-002t`), the entrance record's three-part `bridge` (`am-bm-first-encounter-fjvh`), and `whereItIsTrue` and `intervention` on `Misconception` (`am-read-misconception-callouts-a3o`). Until this round `recap` was cited as that pattern in three places without ever being defined here, so the count said five while the list named four; the field is defined below. Two spellings that diverged before this round are settled here as well, once, for every consumer: `temptingClaims[]` on misconceptions, and lower camel case for the six obstacle kind ids.

**Conventions:** field names are lower camel case; enum values are kebab-case; quantity ids are lower camel case; premise ids follow `<first-author>-<year>-<topic>` (`am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`).

## Scope

In scope: zod schemas, exported types, enums exported as constants for reuse by the reader's labels and the compiler's checks, the dimension basis constant and the rational-exponent shape, the `FoundationLink` shape and typed prerequisites, the structured `WorkedExample` record, the entrance record's `bridge` shape, the `authorship` field on reviewable records, reading target kinds, fixtures.

Out of scope, owned elsewhere:
- dimension arithmetic (`am-cm-dimension-validator-aoz`);
- the expression tree node schema, which narrows this schema's `tree` field (`am-eq-expression-tree-8kl`);
- acyclicity, shelf-date, and edge-type checks (`am-cm-checks-epistemic-o7n`);
- quantity registry content (`am-not-quantity-registry-2f7`);
- card rendering, card-specific validators, and the production publication gate (`am-disc-knowledge-cards-iw8j`);
- the `Journey` composition record, defined only by `am-disc-journey-framework-umbg`, which imports the premise and argument shapes from here; this bead never defines a second journey schema;
- role palette values (`am-design-semantic-color-8vbq`);
- the authorship block's definition (`am-cm-schemas-source-1en`, `src/content/schemas/authorship.ts`) and its compiler checks (`am-edit-review-records-hofz`);
- backlink computation, return-caption validation, and the planned-target rules for foundation links (`am-found-library-infra-002t`), and the prerequisite dead-end audit (`am-found-prereq-audit-71ot`);
- worked-example rendering, the `decisiveStep` disclosure, and the projection-time validation of examples and transfer cases (`am-found-library-infra-002t`), with the phrasing rules for `plausibleFirstThought` registered in `am-edit-voice-lint-trmf`;
- entrance-record rendering, `continueWith` target resolution, the in-preparation state for a `planned` target, and the no-symbol scan over `newSkill` (`am-bm-first-encounter-fjvh`, which reruns `symbolScan` from `am-tours-infra-g518` once that lands);
- misconception rendering and its order, the review-queue report for a `whereItIsTrue` of `none`, and the intervention publication gate (`am-read-misconception-callouts-a3o`);
- the obstacle menu and its labels (`am-read-passage-actions-vbe`) and the clarity payload validator and its aggregate (`am-plat-clarity-signal-nlwr`), both of which import the ids defined here rather than listing them again;
- the map of which bead owns which reading texts, audited by `am-cm-audit-scripts-d34`.

## Requirements

### Four kinds of meaning (shared enums)
- **Logical role:** `definition`, `assumption`, `derivation`, `heuristic-inference`, `empirical-observation`, `qualification`.
- **Historical status:** `available-before-cutoff`, `introduced-in-current-paper`, `later-development`, `pedagogical-reconstruction`.
- **Model status:** `exact-within-model`, `approximation`, `idealized-representation`, `calibrated-empirical-model`, `unsupported-outside-domain`.
- **Execution status:** `static-illustration`, `host-calculation`, `accepted-frankensim-result`, `unavailable-or-refused`.
- `meanings` is an object with four required fields (`logicalRole`, `historicalStatus`, `modelStatus`, `executionStatus`), never one combined flag.

### HistoricalPremise (knowledge card)
The four historical statements are four separate fields:
- **Category 1, publicly available by a date.** `sources[]` (original-evidence citations with locators) and `date`: `{earliest, latest, precision, latestYear}`.
  - `earliest` and `latest` are ISO dates (`YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM`, or `YYYY`), and `precision` (`day`, `month`, `year`) matches their form.
  - `latestYear` is an integer that must equal the year of `latest`; it is the value the audit uses.
- **Category 2, evidence Einstein knew or used it.** `claimsEinsteinKnew: boolean` and `einsteinKnowledgeEvidence[]` (citations), required and non-empty exactly when the claim is made.
- **Category 3, the paper itself cites or asserts it.** `paperCitesOrAsserts[]`, each `{paper, ids[]}` naming the blocks or sentences where the paper cites or asserts the result.
- **Category 4, the site uses it in a reconstruction.** `admittedStages[]` (journey stage anchors `arg-…` permitted to use the card).

Other fields:
- `id` (`<first-author>-<year>-<topic>`, the pattern recorded in `am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`), `proposition` (precise), `limits`, `authorship`, `reviewState`.
- `status`: `available`, `parallel-work`, or `later`.
  - A `later` card (a later confirmation such as Perrin 1909 or Millikan 1916) never appears on a shelf. It appears on the timeline and as evidence in "check it against the world."
- `admittedImport` (a separate optional field): `{fromPaper, resultId, provenance}` for an explicitly admitted 1905 result, such as the relativity paper's §8 light-energy transformation used by the September journey.
  - An admitted-import card has `status: available` and `latestYear: 1905`.
  - The `resultId` must name an `exportedResults` entry of the source paper's manifest (checked by `am-cm-source-manifest-6qa`).
  - The status alone never admits a 1905 result; the `admittedImport` field and the citing journey's declaration do.
- `verification`: `{status: "unverified" | "verified", verifier?, date?, evidenceLocator?, note?}`. When verified, `verifier`, `date`, and `evidenceLocator` (where the original source was checked, for example a page of a pinned scan or a library record) are required. The production publication gate that refuses unverified cards is enforced by `am-disc-knowledge-cards-iw8j`.

No single boolean may stand for more than one of the four statements.

### ArgumentNode and Proof
- **`ArgumentNode`:**
  - `id` (`arg-<paperCode>-<name>`), `paper`, `question`, `conclusion`, `logicalRole`, `derivationChainId`, `limitations`, `meanings`, `authorship`, `reviewState`;
  - `premises[]`, each with `ref` (`{kind: "argument" | "premise" | "equation" | "foundation", id}`) and `edgeType` (`historical-derivation`, `modern-verification-oracle`, `cross-reference`, `pedagogical-reconstruction`);
  - `evidence[]`, each with `ref` (`{kind: "dataset" | "premise" | "citation", id}`), `relation` (`supports`, `tests`, `contradicts`), and `dateLabel` (required when the evidence postdates 1904, for example "later evidence (1909)"). Evidence edges are how a "check it against the world" stage cites Perrin 1909 or Millikan 1916 without placing them on the shelf; the shelf-date rule applies to premises, not to evidence;
  - `sourceSupport[]` (`{paper, id}` references to blocks or sentences) and `prerequisites[]`, typed `{foundationId, kind: "proof-edge" | "cross-link"}` (see foundation links below);
  - `coverageObligation`: `question`, `observableResponse`, `mathematicalOwner`, `nonvisualEquivalent`, and `treatment`, one of `{kind: "instrument", experimentIds[], correspondenceNote?}` (the note is required when an experiment is shared with another node), `{kind: "static", description}`, or `{kind: "omitted", reason}`;
  - `recap`, optional and authored at overview length: what this argument established, for a reader who returns after an interruption. Requested by `am-read-notebook-tde`, additive with a schema-version bump. A node without one validates, because the compiler lists nodes that lack a recap in a coverage report and that report is a warning and never a build failure: the recap card falls back to the node's R0 overview, labeled as the overview. The card, the report, and the fallback belong to `am-read-notebook-tde`; this bead defines the field and rejects an empty string, so a node carries either an authored recap or none.
- **`Proof`:** `id`, `argumentNodeIds[]`, `orderedSteps[]`, `entryAssumptions[]`, `moveTypes[]`, `sourceMapping[]`, `route` (`source-order`, `discovery`, `pedagogical-reconstruction`, `modern-verification`).

### Quantity
One shared field set, used verbatim by `am-not-quantity-registry-2f7`:
- **Identity:** `id` (lower camel case, never a glyph), `name`, `description` (one or two sentences).
- **Dimension:** `dimension` as exact rational exponents over the six-dimension basis of FrankenSim's `fs-qty`. The basis order is the upstream order recorded at the planning audit: length (metre), mass (kilogram), time (second), temperature (kelvin), current (ampere), amount (mole). Each exponent is `{num, den}` with integer `num`, `den > 0`, and lowest terms. The basis constant lives in `src/content/schemas/dimensionBasis.ts`, so the validator and registry import one definition. Luminous intensity is not in the basis because no quantity in the corpus needs it; a record that asks for it is rejected with that explanation.
- **CGS dimensions:** `gaussianDimension` and `emuDimension` (both optional) give rational exponents over the same six-slot basis with the current exponent fixed at zero, because neither CGS system has a base current. Amount and temperature stay available, so per-mole and per-kelvin CGS quantities are expressible.
  - `gaussianDimension` (the Gaussian system, whose electric quantities are electrostatic units) is required for every electromagnetic quantity used in a `gaussian-cgs` equation (charge, current, electric and magnetic field components, potentials, charge and current density). For example, the electron charge is $M^{1/2}L^{3/2}T^{-1}$, a field component is $M^{1/2}L^{-1/2}T^{-1}$, and a potential is $M^{1/2}L^{1/2}T^{-1}$.
  - `emuDimension` (electromagnetic CGS) is required for every electromagnetic quantity used in an `emu-cgs` equation. The light-quanta paper's §8 numerical check is printed in that system, with the gram-equivalent charge $E = 9.6\cdot10^{3}$ and the potential $\Pi\cdot10^{-8}$ read as volts: a charge in abcoulombs is $M^{1/2}L^{1/2}$, the charge of a gram-equivalent is $M^{1/2}L^{1/2}\,\text{mol}^{-1}$, and a potential in abvolts is $M^{1/2}L^{3/2}T^{-2}$.
  - SI, Gaussian, and electromagnetic dimensions of electromagnetic quantities cannot be derived from one another by exponent arithmetic alone, so every system an equation uses is recorded.
- **State-dependent dimension:** `dimensionStatus` is `declared` (the default, which requires `dimension`) or `state-dependent`, which omits `dimension` and requires `dimensionNote`. It exists for symbols whose dimension depends on the unspecified state variables of a general system: paper 2's §2 state variables $p_\nu$, their rates $\varphi_\nu$, the configuration integral $B$, and its factor $J$. A state-dependent quantity has `isConstant: false`, its values are always `symbolic`, and it may be bound only by equation terms and concordance entries, never by experiment parameters, outputs, dataset columns, or constant-set entries. Any dimension check that involves it returns `unsupported-check` naming the quantity.
- **Shape and density:** `mathematicalKind` is `scalar`, `vector`, `vector-component`, or `matrix`. `densityKind` is `density`, `total`, or `not-applicable`, with `densityPer[]` required for `density` (`volume`, `area`, `length`, `frequency-interval`, `wavelength-interval`, `log-interval`, `displacement`, `solid-angle`, `time`).
- **Semantic distinctions dimensions cannot settle:**
  - `spectralBasis`: `per-frequency`, `per-wavelength`, `per-log-interval`, `none`;
  - `frequencyKind`: `cyclic`, `angular`, `not-applicable`;
  - `timeKind`: `coordinate`, `proper`, `not-applicable` (non-relativistic durations use `not-applicable`);
  - `frame`: `stationary-system` (Einstein's $K$, or $(x, y, z)$ in paper 4), `moving-system` ($k$, or $(\xi, \eta, \zeta)$), `object-rest` (the rest frame of the body or particle the quantity describes, called comoving for a particle), `laboratory` (the frame of the apparatus in which the particle moves), `frame-independent`, `not-applicable`;
  - `observation`: `measured`, `latent`, `not-applicable`;
  - `modelArtifact: boolean`, with a required `artifactNote` when true (for example the apparent speed of the velocity trap, which is never a physical velocity);
  - `statistic`: `mean`, `mean-square`, `variance`, `standard-deviation`, `rms`, `none`;
  - `dimensionlessKind`, required when every exponent is zero: `angle`, `hyperbolic-angle`, `ratio`, `count`, `probability`, `pure-number`.
- **Paired quantities** get distinct ids when conflating them is a known error or when they sit on opposite sides of a transformation law. Examples are coordinate versus proper time, comoving versus laboratory force, the same field in the stationary and moving systems, and the rest-frame versus moving-frame body energy. Selecting a vector component ($x$, $y$, $z$) is a term-binding field on the equation, not a separate quantity id.
- **Units and presentation:** `referenceConditions` (for example "per unit frequency interval", "one-dimensional marginal"); `permittedUnits[]` (the canonical SI unit first, then display units, each with an exact rational factor or a stated decimal factor); `formatting` (a precision-policy reference owned by `am-ver-precision-display-5e5`); `colorRole` (one of `energy`, `time-rate`, `space-geometry`, `material`, `statistical`, `field`, `quanta`, matching `am-design-semantic-color-8vbq`).
- **Representation fields:** `representationFields[]` (optional) names the alternative representations a reference evaluator may return for this quantity, for example `logFrequencyEnergyDensity` (the natural logarithm of `frequencyEnergyDensity`, returned with `linearRepresentable: false`). A representation is not a quantity: each name must differ from every registry id (`representation-field-shadows-id` otherwise), and `am-not-quantity-registry-2f7` exempts declared names from its legacy-spelling check so a log-space representation is never mistaken for the different quantity `logIntervalEnergyDensity`.
- **Constants:** `isConstant: boolean` marks a quantity whose values come from constant sets. Exactness and evidential status belong to each constant-set entry, not to the quantity. Einstein's printed $N$ binds `avogadroConstant` with its value from a printed-historical constant set, while an inferred $N$ with an uncertainty binds `avogadroNumberEstimate`; the naming decisions are recorded by `am-not-quantity-registry-2f7`.

### Equation
- **Identity:** `id` (global `eq-<paperCode>-<suffix>`), `anchor` (the local `eq-<suffix>`), `paper`, `section`, `printedNumber?`, `authorship`, `reviewState`.
- **Trees:** `tree` (required; typed as opaque JSON here and narrowed to the node schema by `am-eq-expression-tree-8kl`), `modernTree?`, and `modernRelation` (`rename-only`, `unit-conversion`, `modernization`; required when `modernTree` exists).
- **`notationForms`:** `source` and `modern`, each `{mode: "generated" | "authored", unitSystem: "si" | "gaussian-cgs" | "emu-cgs"}`. An `authored` form also requires `latex` and `termBindings` covering every displayed symbol. `emu-cgs` exists for the light-quanta §8 check as printed; its esu and statvolt restatement is a separate record in `gaussian-cgs`.
- **`terms[]`:** `termId` (`<equationId>.t.<name>`), `quantityId`, `scale?`, `component?` (`x`, `y`, `z`), `role`, `colorRole`. `scale` is an exact rational `{num, den}` (lowest terms, nonzero, default 1) for a printed symbol that denotes a fixed exact multiple of a registered quantity by the paper's own definition. Paper 2's §2 $\kappa$, defined by $2\kappa N = R$, binds `boltzmannConstant` with `scale: {num: 1, den: 2}`, matching the scaled rename of `am-not-concordance-model-uag`.
- **`operations[]`:** `opId` (`<equationId>.op.<name>`), `kind`, `explanation`.
- **Links and readings:** `derivationLinks` (`chainIds[]`, `usedBy[]`); `numericalBindings[]` (`termId`, `experimentId`, `outputId`); `spokenForm` (authored, required, non-empty); `readings` (a `ReadingSet` id); `meanings`.

### Readings and explanation contracts
- `ReadingSet`: `targetId`, `targetKind`, `r0` (overview), `r1` (full explanation, the default), `r2` (show every step), `r3` (historian's margin, with a non-empty `citations[]`), `foundationLinks[]`, `essentialForPrint?` (an optional authored boolean: this target's deeper readings are essential in print even where the print defaults would drop them), `authorship`, `reviewState`. The i18n bead adds language keys. The schema owns the field; `am-plat-print-3orn` owns the print defaults and the compiler check that reads it, so one flag serves print without print authoring content.
  - `targetKind` is one of `paragraph`, `heading`, `footnote`, `closing`, `equation`, `derivation-step`, or `instrument-caption`. It follows the readings ownership ruling: readings beads own paragraph, heading, footnote, and closing readings; equations beads own the readings of displayed equations and derivation steps (the `Equation` entity carries its readings); instrument beads own caption readings.
  - `targetId` must parse as an id of that kind: an alignable unit id for the first four kinds, an equation record id, a derivation step id from `am-eq-derivation-chains-r4c`, or a caption id declared in the instrument's manifest.
- `AuthoringContract` (kept in the editorial record, not shown under every sentence): `question`, `premisesRetained`, `conclusionSupported`, `approximationsIntroduced`, `omissionsAcknowledged`, `bridge`.
  - Each entry of `approximationsIntroduced` and `omissionsAcknowledged` is a typed record `{qualificationId, statement, restricts, scopeCritical}` rather than free prose. A qualification is `scopeCritical` when dropping it changes what the conclusion claims rather than how precisely it is stated. The first three cases are paper 4's additive-constant premise (without it the mass decrease $L/V^2$ is not established), paper 1's Wien-regime restriction (without it the §6 inference reads as a derivation of Planck's law), and paper 3 §10's force convention (without it the transverse-mass expression reads as the modern one).
  - A `scopeCritical` qualification must be stated at R0, R1, and R2, not only at R3. A reader who never opens the historian's margin would otherwise carry away an unrestricted claim, which the engineering doctrine forbids: a simplified account never turns "in this idealized case" into "always". The schema owns the field and `am-cm-audit-scripts-d34` owns the audit that enforces the rule, so one deliverable has one owner.
- `ObstacleResponses` for "What is getting in the way?": `unfamiliarWordOrSymbol`, `algebraicMove`, `physicalReason`, `connectionToPicture`, `purposeOfCalculation`, `tooMuchAtOnce`, plus `exampleFirst` (a worked example reference). Each response may carry `foundationLinks[]`.
  - **One spelling for the six obstacle kinds, and it is lower camel case.** The six ids above are the single spelling in the corpus. They are keys of `ObstacleResponses`, and this bead's convention makes field names lower camel case and reserves kebab case for enum values, so `unfamiliar-word-or-symbol`, `algebraic-move`, `physical-reason`, `connection-to-picture`, `purpose-of-calculation`, and `too-much-at-once` are rejected, each with a message naming the camel-case id that replaces it. The divergence predates this schema and is not introduced by any one bead: `am-read-passage-actions-vbe` lists the kebab forms as the menu's stable kind ids and keys its `obstacleResponses` by them, and that list is the one corrected to these ids.
  - `src/content/schemas/meanings.ts` exports the six as the frozen constant `OBSTACLE_KIND_IDS`, in the order above. `exampleFirst` is deliberately **not** in it: it is a worked-example reference, never an obstacle category and never a payload value, so a consumer that imported "the keys of `ObstacleResponses`" would otherwise acquire a seventh value.
  - `am-plat-clarity-signal-nlwr` imports `OBSTACLE_KIND_IDS` as the vocabulary of its optional `obstacle` payload value and declares no spelling of its own, so the menu, the schema, and the payload validator cannot drift and a rename reaches all three at once. That bead needs no field change under this decision; only the note recording the two unreconciled spellings becomes stale.
- Formalism and guidance are authoring properties behind presets, never extra reader switches (§6.1).

### Foundation and bridge
- `kind`: `foundation` or `bridge` (the zero-assumed-algebra bridges live under `content/foundations/` with `kind: bridge`).
- A **foundation** requires `learningObjective`, `compactExplanation`, `fullExplanation`, `workedExample`, `textualEquivalent`, `prerequisites[]`, `stoppingPoint`, and `returnCaptions[]`.
- A **bridge** requires `concreteOperation`, `compactExplanation`, `textualEquivalent`, `stoppingPoint`, `readinessSign` (what the reader can now explain, never a test), and `returnCaptions[]`.
- Both may carry `instrumentOrConstruction` (an instrument id following the action contract).
- `returnCaptions[]` entries are `{callingAnchor, caption}`; the caption names the quantity and what is held fixed at that calling passage.
- `prerequisites[]` entries are typed `{foundationId, kind: "proof-edge" | "cross-link"}`, as on argument nodes.
- Both kinds carry `authorship`.
- `backlinks` are computed by the compiler. An authored `backlinks` field is rejected.

#### `WorkedExample` has five authored parts

`workedExample` is a structured record, not a paragraph, on a foundation and on a bridge alike. Added at the request of `am-found-library-infra-002t`, additive with a schema-version bump, in the pattern `am-read-notebook-tde` uses for `ArgumentNode.recap`. Five required non-empty parts:

| Part | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `question` | The specific thing this example settles, in one sentence, naming the quantity |
| `given` | The information the reader starts with: the numbers, the units, and what is held fixed |
| `plausibleFirstThought` | One reasonable first move a reader actually makes here, stated without irony, with one sentence on why it is reasonable |
| `decisiveStep` | The calculation or comparison that settles the question, with its arithmetic shown |
| `limitation` | Where the example stops: what it does not show, and what changes at a realistic scale |

- A `workedExample` given as a plain string is rejected with a message naming the five parts, and an empty `limitation` is rejected, because an example with no stated limit is an example claiming to be general.
- `am-found-library-infra-002t` owns the rendering, the `decisiveStep` disclosure behavior, the transfer-case contract, and the projection-time validation; `am-edit-voice-lint-trmf` owns the phrasing rules for `plausibleFirstThought`. This bead defines the shape.

#### The entrance record's `bridge`

A first-encounter record (`entrance-<paper slug>`, whose form and anchor helpers are fixed by `am-cm-id-scheme-8bn`) carries a `bridge` block, and its shape is declared here so one contract covers all four entrances instead of four rendering codebases. Added at the request of `am-bm-first-encounter-fjvh`, additive with a schema-version bump, in the same pattern. This bead defines the `bridge` block only; the rest of the entrance record stays with its authoring beads and the compiler.

| Part | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `newSkill` | The one new thing the reader can now do, named in ordinary words, with no symbol and no notation |
| `whyUsefulHere` | One sentence saying what that skill buys at this paper's argument, naming the section it unlocks |
| `continueWith[]` | Two or three routes onward, each `{target, guidance}`, differing in how much guidance they offer |

- `guidance` is `more-guidance` or `less-guidance`. `target` is a foundation id, a registered instrument, preset or mode id, or a stable content id.
- Fewer than two or more than three `continueWith` entries are rejected: a no-algebra reader meeting six links has been handed a configuration wall.
- A `continueWith[]` whose entries all carry the same `guidance` is rejected. That is the in-record half of `bridge-routes-not-differentiated`; the half that asks whether a route opens an explanation rather than an instrument or a passage needs the target's record and belongs to the compiler.
- Target resolution, the in-preparation rendering of a `planned` target, the report to `am-found-prereq-audit-71ot` and `am-inst-registry-dispatcher-66l0`, and the no-symbol scan over `newSkill` belong to `am-bm-first-encounter-fjvh`. The other three entrance beads supply content against this shape and declare no second contract.

### Foundation links and typed prerequisites
- **`FoundationLink`** is `{foundationId, callingAnchor, returnCaption?}`. It is stored with the calling record and authored by that record's owner: a reading's `foundationLinks[]`, an obstacle response, an equation's or derivation step's tool link, or an instrument caption's reading.
- **Return-caption precedence** (the contract `am-found-library-infra-002t` enforces when it computes backlinks):
  - the link's `returnCaption` is used when present; otherwise the node's `returnCaptions[]` entry for the same `callingAnchor`;
  - a link with neither caption fails the build;
  - a link whose own caption and the node's entry for that anchor both exist and differ fails the build;
  - a node `returnCaptions[]` entry whose anchor has no link is flagged as stale.
- **Typed prerequisites.** A `proof-edge` prerequisite is part of a proof's dependency route: its target must be an authored node, and a `proof-edge` link to a `planned` or unknown id fails the build. A `cross-link` prerequisite is a concept-map link: when its target is `planned`, it renders as "available later" and is reported by `am-found-prereq-audit-71ot` until the node is authored. Cross-link prerequisites, like `cross-reference` premise edges, never enter the acyclicity check of a proof route.

### Misconception
`id`, `paper`, `temptingClaims[]` (one or two claims, each in the words people use), `whyTempting`, `whereItIsTrue`, `whatIsTrue` (R0–R3), `instrumentIds[]` (at least one) or `staticTreatment: {reason}`, `anchors[]` (content ids where it usually arises), `resultIds[]`, `sources[]`, `intervention`, `essentialForPrint?` (the same optional authored boolean, here on the misconception callout, so a callout a paper cannot be understood without survives the print pass), `authorship`, `reviewState`.

- **One spelling, and it is the plural `temptingClaims[]`.** The list holds one or two claims and may not be empty. A ledger entry with two tempting opposites is a real and common case (the relativity ledger's first two entries present both opposites fairly, and `am-read-misconception-callouts-a3o` presents both with equal weight), and a singular field cannot carry the second one without either dropping it or gluing two quotations into one string. The plural subsumes the singular, so a one-claim entry is a one-element list. Three or more claims are rejected: an entry that needs a third claim is two entries. The retired singular `temptingClaim` is rejected with a message naming `temptingClaims[]`. The divergence predates this round; the two beads that still write the singular in their field lists are `am-lq-misconceptions-jenx` and `am-plat-print-3orn`, and they are the records corrected to the plural.
- **`whereItIsTrue`** is an authored paragraph naming the conditions, the quantity, or the regime under which the tempting statement is a correct thing to say, or the literal value `none` with a one-sentence reason when no such case exists. It is required: an absent value is rejected. A value of `none` validates here and is reported by the compiler to the review queue, so a reviewer confirms that no legitimate reading exists. The rendering order (between "why it is tempting" and "what is true") and the review-queue report belong to `am-read-misconception-callouts-a3o`. Added at that bead's request, additive with a schema-version bump, in the pattern `am-read-notebook-tde` uses for `ArgumentNode.recap`.
- **`intervention`** is `{instrumentId, scenarioId, defaultsReviewed, reviewRecordId}`, a reviewed record rather than a rendered field, because the site's own instrument defaults can manufacture the misconception the callout later corrects. `defaultsReviewed` carries four judgments, each a short authored line, covering the model, the labels, the default controls, and the feedback; all four are required, and a record missing one is rejected with that judgment named. `reviewRecordId` names an accepted review record of `am-edit-review-records-hofz`; the schema records the id, and the rule that an agent never sets it, the scope and staleness rules, and the publication gate belong to that bead and to `am-read-misconception-callouts-a3o`. An entry with `staticTreatment` instead of `instrumentIds[]` carries the same four judgments about the static worked example's figure, caption, and numbers, so `instrumentId` and `scenarioId` are optional exactly in that case. Added at the same bead's request, additive with a schema-version bump.

## Technical Approach

`src/content/schemas/argument.ts` holds the zod schemas and inferred types; `src/content/schemas/meanings.ts` exports the enums as constants; `src/content/schemas/dimensionBasis.ts` holds the basis and the rational-exponent schema. Fixtures are YAML files in `src/content/schemas/__fixtures__/argument/`, loaded through the compiler's loaders. `FoundationLink` lives in `argument.ts` and is exported for the reading, equation, derivation-step, and caption schemas. The `authorship` field imports `AuthorshipBlock` from `src/content/schemas/authorship.ts`; citations, review states, and typed dates come from the schemas of `am-cm-schemas-source-1en`.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Schemas, types, and enums exist for every entity and field above, with the listed spellings.
- [ ] The four kinds of meaning are four separate required fields on argument nodes and equations.
- [ ] The four historical statements are four separate fields on knowledge cards, and `status` accepts only `available`, `parallel-work`, and `later`.
- [ ] An admitted-import card must carry `admittedImport`, `status: available`, and `latestYear: 1905`.
- [ ] `edgeType` distinguishes historical derivation from modern verification oracle, and evidence edges are separate from premises.
- [ ] A rational exponent with `den: 0`, a non-integer numerator, or a non-reduced fraction is rejected.
- [ ] A dimensionless quantity without `dimensionlessKind`, a `modelArtifact` without a note, and a request for luminous intensity are rejected.
- [ ] An equation without an authored spoken form is rejected, as are an authored notation form without term bindings and a `modernTree` without `modernRelation`.
- [ ] A misconception without an instrument id and without a static treatment is rejected.
- [ ] Knowledge-card validation:
  - a card with `claimsEinsteinKnew: true` and no evidence is rejected;
  - a verified card without `verifier`, `date`, and `evidenceLocator` is rejected;
  - a card whose `latestYear` differs from the year of `latest`, or whose `precision` does not match its dates, is rejected;
  - a card whose id does not follow `<first-author>-<year>-<topic>` is rejected.
- [ ] A bridge without `concreteOperation` or `readinessSign`, and any record with authored `backlinks`, are rejected.
- [ ] Readings, equation records, argument nodes, foundations and bridges, misconceptions, and knowledge cards without `authorship` are rejected.
- [ ] `FoundationLink` and typed prerequisites validate; a prerequisite without `kind`, or with `kind: "proof"`, is rejected.
- [ ] `ReadingSet.targetKind` accepts exactly the seven kinds, and a `targetId` that does not parse as its kind is rejected.
- [ ] `emu-cgs` is accepted as a unit system, and an `emuDimension` or `gaussianDimension` with a nonzero current exponent is rejected.
- [ ] A `state-dependent` quantity without `dimensionNote`, with a `dimension`, or with `isConstant: true` is rejected.
- [ ] A term `scale` of `{num: 0, den: 1}` or `{num: 2, den: 4}` is rejected, and `{num: 1, den: 2}` is accepted.
- [ ] `essentialForPrint` is optional on reading sets and misconceptions, defaults to absent, and is rejected on any other entity.
- [ ] `representationFields[]` validates, and a representation name equal to a registry id is rejected.
- [ ] `AuthoringContract` qualification entries are typed records carrying `scopeCritical`, and a fixture contract whose scope-changing qualification is marked reaches the audit with that flag intact.
- [ ] `ArgumentNode.recap` is optional and, when present, non-empty: a node with an authored recap and a node without one both validate, and a `recap` given as an empty string is rejected.
- [ ] `WorkedExample` requires all five parts on foundations and on bridges; a plain-string worked example and an empty `limitation` are rejected, the first with the five part names in the message.
- [ ] An entrance `bridge` requires `newSkill`, `whyUsefulHere`, and `continueWith[]`; two and three routes validate, one route and four routes are rejected, and a `continueWith[]` whose entries all carry the same `guidance` is rejected.
- [ ] `Misconception.temptingClaims[]` accepts one or two claims; an empty list, three claims, and the retired singular `temptingClaim` are rejected, the last with a message naming `temptingClaims[]`.
- [ ] A misconception without `whereItIsTrue` is rejected, and the literal `none` with its reason is accepted.
- [ ] `intervention` requires `reviewRecordId` and all four `defaultsReviewed` judgments; a record missing one judgment is rejected with that judgment named, and an entry with `staticTreatment` validates without `instrumentId` and `scenarioId`.
- [ ] The six obstacle kind ids are exported as the frozen constant `OBSTACLE_KIND_IDS` in lower camel case, `exampleFirst` is not among them, and a kebab-case obstacle key is rejected with a message naming the camel-case id.

