**One compiler suite, one directory.** Every check family runs in one compiler process, so the compiler writes a single diagnostics file per run at `artifacts/test-logs/build-content/<log-run-id>.jsonl` with `suite: "build-content"` on every line, following the logger mapping for scripts (the suite is the script name, `scripts/build-content.ts`). The family is the validated field `extra.family`, taken from the registered check. This bead reports under `extra.family: "manifest"`. The declared families are `compiler`, `structural`, `semantic`, `epistemic`, `manifest`, `dimension`, `coverage`, `readings`, `audit`, `anachronism`, `exports`, `i18n`, `ids`, `schema`, `notation`, and `voice`, and `registerCheck` refuses any other, so no check can invent a suite name or a second directory. Test suites keep their own names and directories: this bead's unit and integration suite logs `suite: "source-manifest-tests"` to `artifacts/test-logs/source-manifest-tests/<log-run-id>.jsonl`. No suite writes into another suite's directory, and no path spells the run id in camel case.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- PDF covers or editorial matter can shift PDF page indices away from journal pages; always validate against the asset's page map.
- Footnotes, references, and date-lines are easy to forget; the inventory beads use this check as their safety net.
- Do not let `machine-draft` statuses count toward completeness.
- A naive "one unit per page" or "pages never decrease" rule rejects correct inventories; test the span and footnote cases explicitly.
- Chronology of imports is an epistemic safeguard as well as a structural one: a paper can only import what was already written.
- Checking gaps in inline math or reference indices would punish the rule that keeps ids stable; those indices are allowed to skip.
- An export identified by the plan's compact $\beta$ form would point at a formula the paper may never print; identify it by the ledger's printed LaTeX.

