### Structured logging
As in requirement 4; failures include the scenario file path and a one-line reproduction command, and each keeps the failure file named in its record. A failing `rounds-to` row writes, under `artifacts/test-logs/scenarios/<log-run-id>/failures/<scenarioId>.json`, the printed value and its precision, the derived interval, the full-precision recomputation, the constant set, the declared editorial inputs, and the reproduction command. A failing illustrative-input refusal writes the entry, its `evidentialRole`, the path it was reached by, and the entry the message names instead. A failing dataset-inference refusal writes the dataset id, the model id, and the admitted list. A failing identity writes both routes' owner ids, both results, and the import-graph finding. A failing or `indeterminate` discrimination case retains both hypotheses' full outputs, the computed difference, the tolerance spec, and the reproduction command.

## Considerations and Pitfalls

- Keep historical constants exactly as printed; a scenario that silently uses modern constants to "reproduce" Einstein proves nothing.
- An editorial input is honest only when it is declared; an undeclared $R$ borrowed from another paper looks like a transcription, and a declared one that drifts from its constant set is worse, because both records look right alone.
- Never "fix" a suspected misprint silently: the source face keeps the original and the scenario records the correction.
- Never loosen a tolerance to pass; changing a tolerance requires a rationale change in the file and review.
- Stochastic scenarios use registered stream allocations, never ad hoc seeds.
- Three printings are three fixtures with three constant sets; one data set cannot produce $2.1\times10^{23}$ and $6.56\times10^{23}$, because the coefficient of $\varphi$ moves $N$ only by $\sqrt{2.5} = 1.5811388$.
- A golden scenario proves plumbing, not physics; the labels say so.
- Reading Einstein's printed 0.8 micron as an input is the most natural mistake in this registry, because the number is right there in the constant set and it is exactly the value the fixture is about. It is the output, and a fixture that consumed it would reproduce his number from his number while every check passed.
- The illustrative-input refusal and the derived-value flag look alike and mean different things. One says a calculation would be circular; the other says a human should look. Collapsing them would either block honest fixtures or hide circular ones.
- A dataset's admitted-model list is a statement about what its provenance supports, not about what the numbers permit arithmetically. A scenario can always compute an inference from a table of numbers; the list is what says whether the result would mean anything.
- An empty admitted-model list is a decision and must refuse every inference. Treating it as "unset" would turn the most careful dataset records into the most permissive ones.
- A rounding interval is not a tolerance and must not be converted into one. "0,8 Mikron" carries one significant figure, and [0.75, 0.85) is a statement about what the printer set, not about numerical error.
- Take `printedPrecision` from the printed string, never from the recomputation. A precision read off the computed value will always pass and will always be wrong.
- "ca." is the source hedging, not permission to widen. Record it, and widen only with explicit bounds and a stated reason that a reviewer can see.
- Two routes written by the same author from the same line of the same paper are weakly independent. Prefer a distinct derivation, an exact special case, or a separately implemented numerical method, and say in `routes[].label` which of the three it is.
- An engine comparison is not an identity check. Running the same derivation in Rust and in TypeScript proves transport and arithmetic, not mathematics.
- A `discrimination` scenario is not a way to avoid a conclusion. Every one of them must be able to report `discriminates`, and at least one shipped scenario must actually do so, or the kind becomes a place to file awkward comparisons.
- Two hypotheses that share an owner function agree by construction. The same-owner rejection is the whole point of the kind; without it this is a slower way of printing one number twice.
- "Indistinguishable" is always "under this observation, at this tolerance." Never render it without both, and never let a scenario inherit a tolerance from another scenario.
- Do not migrate `am-reason-countermodel-workbench-t07q`'s cases into scenarios. That workbench is a reader-facing matrix with its own case-file contract and stop rule; this kind is an instrument's own acceptance case. They may share owner functions and must not share files.
- Widening a tolerance to turn `indeterminate` into `indistinguishable` is weakening a gate, which AGENTS.md forbids without a recorded reason. The boundary result is information, not an obstacle.
- The kind adds no output status, so `statusEnumIds`, the strict decoder, and the voice lint's leak list are untouched. Keep it that way: if a later change wants a per-output non-discrimination status, it belongs in `am-rt-typed-results-mqb` with all four of its consumers updated in the same change.

